WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.009
Open up the shell of a heat exchanger

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:04.181
and you find plates standing in the way.

00:00:04.481 --> 00:00:05.882
Why would anyone put walls

00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:07.175
in the path of the flow?

00:00:07.675 --> 00:00:10.340
Take them out, and the shell-side fluid takes

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.181
the easy road: straight along the tubes, slowly.

00:00:13.481 --> 00:00:16.014
Slow flow leaves a still layer on the tubes,

00:00:16.014 --> 00:00:17.568
and heat crosses it poorly.

00:00:17.868 --> 00:00:19.612
And long tubes with nothing to

00:00:19.612 --> 00:00:21.241
hold them can sag and shake.

00:00:21.741 --> 00:00:24.231
Baffles are plates with one edge cut away,

00:00:24.231 --> 00:00:25.831
alternating top and bottom.

00:00:26.131 --> 00:00:27.953
They force the fluid to zigzag

00:00:27.953 --> 00:00:30.017
across the tubes, again and again.

00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:33.083
Flowing across the tubes, it moves faster and

00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:35.603
scrubs the surface, so heat moves better.

00:00:35.903 --> 00:00:37.813
The plates also hold the tubes up,

00:00:37.813 --> 00:00:38.936
so they stay steady.

00:00:39.436 --> 00:00:41.101
But every turn costs pressure.

00:00:41.401 --> 00:00:44.415
Halve the baffle spacing:

00:00:44.415 --> 00:00:48.513
shell-side coefficient about 1.5×.

00:00:48.813 --> 00:00:51.481
Pressure drop: about 7×.

00:00:51.981 --> 00:00:55.128
Usual spacing: 1/5 of the shell

00:00:55.128 --> 00:00:57.768
diameter up to 1 diameter.

00:00:58.268 --> 00:01:01.076
Today, engineers try many baffle layouts in

00:01:01.076 --> 00:01:03.689
rating software before any steel is cut,

00:01:03.689 --> 00:01:06.628
weighing heat transfer against pressure drop.

00:01:07.128 --> 00:01:09.406
Baffles trade pressure for heat transfer,

00:01:09.406 --> 00:01:10.852
and keep the tubes steady.

00:01:11.352 --> 00:01:14.137
These numbers are an illustrative worked example.

00:01:14.637 --> 00:01:16.750
Next: one fluid goes in the tubes,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.925
one in the shell. Which goes where?
